#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157109 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca43484-4a4f-57b7-a4c2-23d1db997afd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "8831d548-7068-5c95-940d-c572f229c23b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7901" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$\\dfrac{2}{5x^4 +2} \\lt \\dfrac{5-2x}{5x^4+2}$." :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator $5x^4+ 2$ is positive for all values of $x$. So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $5x^4+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5x^{4} + 2} & < \\frac{5 - 2x}{5x^{4} + 2} \\\\ (5x^{4} + 2) \\cdot \\frac{2}{5x^{4} + 2} & < (5x^{4} + 2) \\cdot \\frac{5 - 2x}{5x^{4} + 2} \\\\ 2 & < 5 - 2x \\\\ 2x & < 3 \\\\ x & < \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef29dc50-df8b-50b7-9533-c670e3c08347" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Division by a Positive Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "ecf42887-e079-5c33-99f8-d59fb138cba9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7902" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$6x^3 + 3x \\lt 0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x^{3} + 3x & < 0. \\\\ 3x(2x^{2} + 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $2x^2 + 1$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(3x(2x^{2} + 1))/(2x^{2} + 1) & < \\frac{0}{2x^{2} + 1} \\\\ 3x & < 0 \\\\ x & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b438f1-77af-551c-8a45-52a93e5995a8" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Requiring Multiplication or Division by a Negative Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "10d11958-5339-54b2-a468-fb97f0752a75" :question/math-academy-id "e-7903" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$(2\\sin{x}-9)(2x) \\leq 0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that the factor $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad -11 \\leq 2\\sin x - 9 \\leq -7\n$$\n\nBecause $2\\sin{x}-9$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2\\sin x - 9)(2x) & \\le 0 \\\\ ((2\\sin x - 9)(2x))/(2\\sin x - 9) & \\ge \\frac{0}{2\\sin x - 9} \\\\ 2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/id #uuid "684b599a-3f29-5978-8a2c-140186e56c29" :question/math-academy-id "e-7904" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\dfrac{x+5}{1-3x} \\lt 2, \\quad x \\lt 0$." :question/worked-solution "Since $x\\lt 0$, we have that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & < 0 \\\\ -x & > 0 \\\\ -3x & > 0 \\\\ 1 - 3x & > 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nThis means that $1-3x$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x\\lt 0)$. So, we can multiply both sides by $1-3x$, and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < 2 \\\\ (1 - 3x) \\cdot \\frac{x + 5}{1 - 3x} & < (1 - 3x) \\cdot 2 \\\\ x + 5 & < 2 - 6x \\\\ 7x & < - 3 \\\\ x & < - \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x \\lt 0$ and\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\lt -\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bdbe888-6d00-5139-b424-b4378f4e774a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 < x < \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156737} {:answer/id #uuid "a9350c50-68ba-5235-b532-72562a07406c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 < x < 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156738} {:answer/id #uuid "bf87081e-0c4f-5601-a029-f5e86af1d101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156739} {:answer/id #uuid "45a87c60-7da5-5b7c-8371-1f060b294205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156740} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3e006a-7310-52d5-bb92-33e7abb66360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a87c60-7da5-5b7c-8371-1f060b294205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156740} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a302d4-a2cf-52a7-8e7a-eae757ccb440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156736}] :question/id #uuid "69f3af74-324d-5931-85f9-57e1b17f2095" :question/math-academy-id "q-89117" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n\n$\\frac{3x + 2}{1 - x} > 4, x > 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e4aa85c-345d-5869-9dcd-b2ef128dae3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inequalities Involving Domain Restrictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8f7eacd-7832-5a05-8044-eeb1810c5d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x \\ge 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156785} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa40aa9-ccf0-5db8-8469-3f5024e4aa53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x > 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156786} {:answer/id #uuid "049cd968-95bb-50d5-b4d8-17064bba4ba2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer